/**
 * @author: Karley W.
 *
 * This file contains code for carving both contiguous and fragmented ELF files.
 *
 * ELF files typically follow this format:
 *
 *   [ELF Header]
 *   [Program Header Table]
 *   [Section 1]
 *   [Section 2]
 *   ...
 *   [Section N]
 *   [Section Header Table]
 *
 * However, as long as the ELF Header appears at the beginning, the other
 * components can appear in any order, and many components (including the
 * section header table) are optional.
 *
 *
 * CONTIGUOUS CARVING
 * ------------------
 * The contiguous carver handles ELF files whose blocks appear sequentially
 * on disk. It relies on the Section Header Table (SHT) to compute the total
 * file size. If the SHT is absent, the Program Header Table (PHT) is used
 * to make a best-effort size estimate.
 *
 * The carver can recover executables up to a configurable maximum size
 * (scalpelconf.c; default 256 MB). Setting this too large can cause memory
 * problems; setting it too small will cause valid files to be missed. If
 * memory pressure is an issue, use the -a flag to disable aggressive
 * memory allocation. Note that because ELF has no footer, all bytes up to
 * the maximum size must be read from the header position and held in memory
 * for every candidate until the validator can compute the true size and
 * truncate it. This makes the ELF carver significantly more memory-intensive
 * than most other supported file types.
 *
 *
 * FRAGMENTED REASSEMBLY
 * ---------------------
 * The reassembly engine (elf_reassembly() and its call tree) recovers ELF
 * files whose blocks are non-contiguous on disk. It operates in four
 * broad phases:
 *
 *   1. Skeleton initialization (elf_reassembly_init_candidate /
 *      build_candidate_state_64/32):
 *      The SHT and PHT are parsed from the first recovered block to
 *      derive a ground-truth FileState: the expected file offset, size,
 *      and section type of every component (ELF header, PHT, SHT, .dynamic,
 *      .text, relocations, strings, etc.). This skeleton drives all
 *      subsequent placement decisions.
 *
 *   2. Exact relocation-anchor recovery:
 *      RELA addend signatures are matched globally to locate fragments that
 *      moved as a unit. A proposed signed-displacement run is assembled in
 *      scratch storage and accepted only when the complete physical mapping
 *      passes the ELF validator.
 *
 *   3. Block placement loop (elf_reassembly):
 *      The engine iterates over unfilled slots in the candidate BlockVector,
 *      prioritizing section types in a fixed order (DYNAMIC, RELOCATION,
 *      SYMBOL, STRING, GOT, PLT, EH_FRAME_HEADER, EH_FRAME, TEXT). For
 *      each unfilled slot, it runs a windowed seed-and-preview search
 *      (elf_reassembly_get_block_choice) to find the best apparent block
 *      number to place there. The search evaluates candidates using a
 *      coverage-based logit scorer (compute_block_logit_for_section) with
 *      locality bonuses, cross-validation evidence, and damping, all
 *      governed by the tunable ElfTunables bundle. After each successful
 *      placement, a gallop pass (elf_reassembly_gallop / elf_try_gallop_guarded)
 *      greedily extends the run in both directions by committing
 *      sequentially-scored strong blocks, with bounded weak-streak staging
 *      to tolerate short ambiguous stretches.
 *
 *   4. Final .text fill (elf_reassembly_fill_text_holes_final):
 *      After the main loop, any remaining unfilled slots whose ground-truth
 *      expectation is purely SECTION_TEXT are handled by a separate greedy
 *      pass. This pass collects globally available .text-like fragments
 *      (contiguous runs of FULL/PARTIAL/UNKNOWN blocks in actual-block space)
 *      and matches them to holes of equal length. Standard .eh_frame_hdr
 *      function starts and direct-call targets can identify a statistically
 *      separated fragment; otherwise the fragment whose center is closest to
 *      neighboring placed blocks remains the fallback.
 *      The completed physical layout is checked against the SHT-derived
 *      section expectations. A fragmented mapping remains PROMISING because
 *      ELF has no general integrity check for opaque displaced bytes.
 *
 * Block and file state are persisted across reassembly calls via the
 * block_get_state / block_put_state and carve_get_state / carve_put_state
 * hashtable APIs (BlockState and FileState respectively). BlockState records
 * the section components and layout identified within a single disk block.
 * FileState records the full skeleton, placement progress, active search
 * state (windowed seed scan, gallop resume cursor, failed-gallop guard), and
 * the seed top-M retention table used by the preview scorer.
 *
 * The ONNX inference model (elf_onnx_global.h) provides per-block section
 * classification scores that feed into the coverage matcher at block
 * validation time (elf_classify_block).
 *
 * All numeric thresholds governing placement acceptance, gallop behavior,
 * search window sizes, and preview scoring are collected in ElfTunables
 * (default values in default_tunables()) to allow calibration and
 * sweeping without source changes.
 **/


#pragma GCC diagnostic push
#pragma GCC diagnostic ignored "-Wunused-variable"
#pragma GCC diagnostic ignored "-Wunused-but-set-variable"
#pragma GCC diagnostic ignored "-Wunused-function"
#pragma GCC diagnostic ignored "-Wunused-parameter"
#pragma GCC diagnostic ignored "-Wtype-limits"

